1
00:00:00,031 --> 00:00:06,718
Have you ever wondered why modern servers can have a 100 gigabit network card, but still struggle to process every packet at line rate?

2
00:00:07,459 --> 00:00:09,041
The answer isn't that Linux is slow.

3
00:00:09,602 --> 00:00:13,806
It's that the traditional networking path does a lot of work for every single packet.

4
00:00:14,567 --> 00:00:15,668
Let's see what actually happens.

5
00:00:16,469 --> 00:00:17,911
A packet arrives at the network card.

6
00:00:18,492 --> 00:00:26,140
Instead of your application receiving it immediately, the network card first raises an interrupt to tell the CPU, a packet has arrived.

7
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:31,166
The CPU stops whatever it was doing, switches into kernel mode, and starts running the network driver.

8
00:00:32,027 --> 00:00:33,789
That interruption alone has a cost.

9
00:00:34,690 --> 00:00:38,294
Next, the packet travels through multiple layers of the Linux networking stack.

10
00:00:39,075 --> 00:00:47,705
During this journey, the packet is wrapped in kernel data structures, inspected, and often copied into different memory buffers before it finally reaches your application.

11
00:00:48,466 --> 00:00:51,790
And that's another source of overhead – memory copies.

12
00:00:51,790 --> 00:00:58,223
Copying thousands or even millions of packets every second consumes valuable CPU cycles and memory bandwidth.

13
00:00:59,024 --> 00:01:02,090
Finally, your application has to wake up to process the packet.

14
00:01:02,731 --> 00:01:06,238
That means another context switch, from the kernel back to user space.

15
00:01:06,879 --> 00:01:12,310
Context switches save and restore CPU state, flush caches, and add more latency.

16
00:01:12,310 --> 00:01:17,600
Now imagine this happening not once, but millions of times every second.

17
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:20,205
Interrupts, memory copies, context switches.

18
00:01:20,966 --> 00:01:22,349
Individually, they seem small.

19
00:01:22,810 --> 00:01:26,336
Together, they become the biggest bottleneck in high-performance networking.

20
00:01:27,057 --> 00:01:29,522
This is where DPDK changes everything.

21
00:01:29,522 --> 00:01:33,690
Instead of waiting for interrupts, DPDK continuously polls the network card.

22
00:01:34,351 --> 00:01:43,288
It maps packet buffers directly into user space memory using huge pages, allowing the application to access packets without the traditional kernel networking stack.

23
00:01:43,849 --> 00:01:44,290
The result?

24
00:01:44,971 --> 00:01:50,802
No per-packet interrupts, no unnecessary memory copies, almost no context switches.

25
00:01:50,802 --> 00:01:55,532
The CPU spends its time processing packets instead of managing operating system overhead.

26
00:01:56,354 --> 00:02:04,231
That's why technologies like DPDK power high-performance firewalls, load balancers, telecom systems, and modern cloud networking.

27
00:02:04,872 --> 00:02:10,704
Where processing millions of packets per second isn't just an optimization, it's a requirement.

28
00:02:10,724 --> 00:02:15,675
Once you understand where the time is actually spent, the performance difference becomes obvious.
